modelAnswer:
Disaccharides form when two monosaccharides condense, releasing one water molecule.
Common types: sucrose, made of glucose plus fructose, found in sugarcane and sugar beet;
maltose, glucose plus glucose, produced during seed germination and in germinating barley;
lactose, glucose plus galactose, present in mammalian milk.
All three are white crystalline solids, sweeter than polysaccharides but less sweet than their parent monosaccharides.
